  "_comment": "Reference values for ASE-DFTFE automated tests. Energy in Hartree, forces in Ha/Bohr. null = NOT pinned: the suite FAILS on it. Seed deliberately with `test_suite.py --seed` on a binary you trust, never by accident.",
  "_validated": "Host arm, np=64: 6/6 PASS on 2026-08-14 (jobs 8755816 + 8755829). Five of six agreed with native pGD BITWISE on energy and forces; al_slab_semiperiodic, the one case carrying a rigid placement shift, agreed to 2.6e-13 Ha / 4.9e-12 Ha/Bohr. relax_o2's relaxed landing point sat 3.265e-09 Ha from native LBFGS, i.e. at the MPI-reduction noise floor.",
  "_binary_note": "The pGD CPU binaries that GENERATED these numbers were replaced on 2026-08-14 12:29/12:34 by a rebuild after the PR-714 (LDOS) merge, so the exact executables are no longer on disk. The numbers stand -- provenance records the binary but deliberately does not gate on it, and PR 714 is LDOS/Helmholtz work that none of these six cases exercise. But if a future host run FAILS by a small margin, suspect that rebuild before suspecting the interface, and re-generate with make_references.py rather than loosening a tolerance.",
  "_schema": "test_suite.py gates on three keys per case: 'energy_ha' (float), 'forces_ha_per_bohr' (N x 3 list) and 'stress_ha_per_bohr3' (3 x 3 list, DFT-FE's own units and shape as printed under 'Cell stress (Hartree/Bohr^3)'; null where the case does not request stress -- only al_bulk_periodic does). ASE reports stress as Voigt-6 in eV/A^3; the suite converts the computed value into these units, never the reference out of them. Stress is the most placement-dependent quantity here (PROJECT.md 14.2), so a stress reference is only comparable to a run at the same placement. A case may also carry a 'relaxed' block ('energy_ha', 'forces_ha_per_bohr', 'force_tol_ha_bohr', 'native_steps') -- that one is REPORTED, never gated: it is native LBFGS's landing point against ASE BFGS's, so its achievable tolerance is measured, not asserted. For a relaxation case the gated 'energy_ha' is the single point at the INITIAL geometry.",
